<Chapter id="resume">
<h2>Sunken Resume</h2>
  <p>Resume starts after the Front handoff. Sunken waits for the client to send
  <code>Auth/1</code> as the first record.</p>

  <h3 id="resume-name">Resume</h3>
  <p><b>Resume is a Blizzard type name.</b> The BSN metadata contains the types
  <code>Battlenet::Client::Authentication::ResumeRequest</code> and
  <code>Battlenet::Client::Authentication::ResumeResponse</code>. Client-to-server
  <code>Auth/1</code> selects ResumeRequest. Server-to-client <code>Auth/1</code>
  selects ResumeResponse.</p>

  <p><b>Resume continues the authenticated session context.</b> Front authenticates
  the Battle.net account. Front selects the SC2 game account. GameUtilities returns
  the Sunken endpoint and the session data. Resume proves possession of this data
  on the new connection.</p>

  <table>
    <caption>Native authentication entry points named in the embedded metadata</caption>
    <thead><tr><th>Route</th><th>Embedded request type</th><th>Starting context</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>Auth/0</code></td><td><code>LogonRequest3</code></td><td>Cold native account logon; asks the native service to choose account-logon modules.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>Auth/1</code></td><td><code>ResumeRequest</code></td><td>Continues the account and game-account session authorized by the Front handoff. This is the path documented here.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>Auth/17</code></td><td><code>SingleSignOnRequest3</code></td><td>Begins from a native SSO identifier supplied by another trusted context.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3 id="resume-request">Auth/1 ResumeRequest</h3>
  <p>Logical payload definition:</p>
  <RustSchema code={`struct ResumeRequest {
    common: RequestCommon,
    account: Vec<u8>,
    game_account_region: u8, // from Front LogonResponse3
    game_account_name: Vec<u8>,
}

struct RequestCommon {
    program: FourCC,         // "S2"
    platform: FourCC,        // "Mc64"
    locale: FourCC,          // "enUS"
    versions: [Version; 5], // values in the table below
}`} />
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Program</th><th>Component/platform</th><th>Version</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>S2</code></td><td><code>NGD1</code></td><td><code>0x5bc8dcc1</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>S2</code></td><td><code>NGD2</code></td><td><code>0xfade3a32</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>S2</code></td><td><code>NGD3</code></td><td><code>0x0c129365</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>S2</code></td><td><code>NGD4</code></td><td><code>0x86b7c0ed</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>Bnet</code></td><td><code>Mc64</code></td><td><code>0x000a16a7</code> (661159)</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>These values apply to Base97563. The native compatibility number is an
  independent protocol constant. The Front LogonRequest separately sends
  <code>application_version=0</code>.</p>
  <p>The client must send all four <code>S2/NGD*</code> records followed by the exact
  <code>Bnet/Mc64</code> record shown above.</p>

  <h3 id="resume-proof">Proof exchange</h3>
  <figure class="sequence">
<pre is:raw><span class="syn-direction">C -&gt; S</span>  <span class="syn-route">Auth/1</span>   <span class="syn-field">ResumeRequest</span>
<span class="syn-direction">S -&gt; C</span>  <span class="syn-route">Auth/18</span>  <span class="syn-field">Configuration</span>
<span class="syn-direction">S -&gt; C</span>  <span class="syn-route">Auth/2</span>   <span class="syn-field">ProofRequest</span> <span class="syn-comment">[two modules]</span>
<span class="syn-direction">C -&gt; S</span>  <span class="syn-route">Auth/2</span>   <span class="syn-field">ProofResponse</span> <span class="syn-comment">[one session-proof output]</span>
<span class="syn-direction">S -&gt; C</span>  <span class="syn-route">Auth/1</span>   <span class="syn-field">ResumeResponse</span> <span class="syn-comment">[phase-two server proof]</span>
</pre>
  </figure>
  <p>The Resume ProofRequest contains two 40-byte module IDs. Each ID contains an
  eight-byte usage prefix and a 32-byte module identity.</p>
  <ol>
    <li><b>Thumbprint module.</b> The server sends a 4096-bit RSA PKCS#1 v1.5
    SHA-512 signature. Hash the 16-byte peer address and then the ASCII domain
    <code>Thumbprint.IPv6</code>. Convert an IPv4 address to IPv4-mapped IPv6. Verify
    the signature with exponent 65537 and the SC2 public modulus. The wire signature
    is little-endian. Reverse its 512 bytes before RSA verification. Verification
    occurs locally and yields zero <code>ModuleOutput</code> elements.</li>
    <li><b>Session-proof module.</b> The server data is
    <code>00 || server_nonce[16]</code>. Use the 64-byte GameUtilities value as seed
    <code>B</code>. Generate <code>client_nonce[16]</code>. Calculate key
    <code>C</code> with the formula below. Send
    <code>01 || client_nonce || HMAC-SHA256(C,
    00 || client_nonce || server_nonce)</code>. Verify the proof in the successful
    ResumeResponse.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>For GameUtilities seed <code>B</code>, client nonce <code>Nc</code>, and
  server nonce <code>Ns</code>, the Resume ratchet and proofs are:</p>
<pre is:raw><span class="syn-field">C</span> = <span class="syn-type">HMAC-SHA256</span>(B, <span class="syn-number">0x00</span> || Nc || Ns)
 || <span class="syn-type">HMAC-SHA256</span>(B, <span class="syn-number">0x01</span> || Ns || Nc)

<span class="syn-field">client_proof</span>    = <span class="syn-type">HMAC-SHA256</span>(C, <span class="syn-number">0x00</span> || Nc || Ns)
<span class="syn-field">server_proof</span>    = <span class="syn-type">HMAC-SHA256</span>(C, <span class="syn-number">0x01</span> || Ns || Nc)
<span class="syn-field">transport_secret</span> = C
</pre>
  <p>Resume first converts seed <code>B</code> to <code>C</code>. Auth/2 proofs and
  transport-key derivation use <code>C</code>. The domain-two KDF belongs to the
  full-logon handshake.</p>
  <p>An optional plaintext <code>Conn/11 RegulatorUpdate</code> can appear before
  <code>Auth/1 ResumeResponse</code>.</p>

  <h3 id="protection">Stream protection</h3>
  <p>The client must validate the server proof. The client then sends an empty
  <code>Conn/5 EnableEncryption</code> record in plaintext. Both directions then use
  stateful RC4 beginning with the first keystream byte.</p>
<pre is:raw><span class="syn-field">inbound_key</span>  = <span class="syn-type">HMAC-SHA256</span>(transport_secret,
                <span class="syn-number">68e0c72eddd6d2f31e5ab155b18b631e</span>)
<span class="syn-field">outbound_key</span> = <span class="syn-type">HMAC-SHA256</span>(transport_secret,
                <span class="syn-number">dea965ae543a1e939e690caa68de7839</span>)
</pre>
  <p>The inbound and outbound RC4 states are independent. Each state continues across
  TCP operations and Sunken record boundaries, independent of TCP segmentation.</p>
  <p>One receive operation can contain a plaintext ResumeResponse and protected
  bytes. In this case, enable the inbound state after ResumeResponse. Decrypt the
  protected suffix one time.</p>
  <p>After protection starts, the connection starts the native service bootstrap.</p>
  <BackToContents />
</Chapter>
